<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Shimonoseki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a>, signed on 17 April 1895, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> recognised the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a> Islands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> Peninsula to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and paid a large indemnity, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-intervention">Triple Intervention</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> soon forced Japan to return Liaodong. The treaty marked Japan's emergence as an imperial power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki.jpg" alt="Treaty of Shimonoseki" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japan_China_Peace_Treaty_17_April_1895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
